---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `lives = 3

while lives > 0:
    print("You lost a life!")

print("Game over.")`;

const Bug2 = `print("Counting to 10:")

for number in range(1, 10):
    print(number)`;

const Bug3 = `print("Adding up your scores.")

for score in [4, 8, 15]:
    total = 0
    total = total + score

print("Total:", total)`;

const Bug4 = `for round_num in range(1, 4):
    print("Round", round_num)
print("  You scored a point!")`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Loops</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four broken loops. None of them crash. One of them never stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            These are the four loop bugs you are most likely to write yourself
            during Group Project 1, so it is worth being able to recognise all four
            on sight.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Recognising why a loop never ends</li>
            <li>Checking what <code>range()</code> actually produces</li>
            <li>Knowing what belongs inside the loop and what belongs before it</li>
            <li>Reading indentation as meaning</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="warning">
          <h2>Before You Run Bug 1</h2>

          <p>
            Bug 1 is an <b class="text-sn-red">infinite loop</b>. It will print
            forever and will not stop on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Press <b class="text-sn-theme">Ctrl + C</b> in the terminal to kill it.
            Get used to that now &mdash; every programmer writes an infinite loop
            eventually, and knowing how to stop one is part of the job.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <p>
            For each one: what is wrong, and what is the corrected line? All four
            are logic errors, so no traceback is coming to help you.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> prints <b class="text-sn-red"
              >You lost a life!</b
            > forever. <b>Game over.</b> never prints.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-1.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The condition on line 3 is correct. Ask yourself what would ever have
            to change for it to become false, and whether anything in the loop
            changes it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> counts <b class="text-sn-red">1 through 9</b>. The 10
            never appears.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-2.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            An off-by-one. Write down exactly which numbers{" "}
            <code>range(1, 10)</code> produces, then fix it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> prints <b class="text-sn-red">Total: 15</b>. The three
            scores add up to <b class="text-sn-green">27</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-3.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Notice that 15 is the <i>last</i> score in the list. That is the clue.
            One line is in the wrong place &mdash; which line, and where should it
            go instead?
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is the single most common loop bug there is.</b> Once you have
            seen it, you will recognise it for the rest of your life.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> prints all three rounds, then{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">You scored a point!</b> exactly once at the end,
            instead of once per round.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="bug-4.py"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The extra spaces at the start of that message make it <i>look</i> like
            it belongs to the loop. Looking indented and being indented are not the
            same thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong with each</li>
            <li>The corrected line, and where it belongs</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Then this: <b
              >Bugs 3 and 4 are both about a line being in the wrong place. What is
              the difference between them?</b
            >
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
